{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CgfbwmGytDvmLWN9Wjvv2","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"For tetrahedral Co(II) (d⁷), **Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹ (Δt = 4070 cm⁻¹) and B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹**. I worked this out by hand with the standard ligand-field formulas and did not use the fitting tools.\n\n**Approach**\n\nTetrahedral d⁷ has the same quartet energy pattern as octahedral d³ (the \"hole\" equivalence). The ground state is ⁴A₂. The three spin-allowed bands are assigned as:\n\n- ν₁: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₂ = 10Dq = 4070 cm⁻¹\n- ν₂: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(F) = 7030 cm⁻¹\n- ν₃: ⁴A₂ → ⁴T₁(P) = 16 400 cm⁻¹\n\nThe two ⁴T₁ levels mix with each other. Their energies are:\n\nE(⁴T₁) = 15Dq + 7.5B ∓ ½·√(225B² + 100Dq² − 180·B·Dq)\n\n**Solving**\n\n1. **Find Dq from ν₁.** 10Dq = 4070, so Dq = 407 cm⁻¹ (Δt = 4070 cm⁻¹).\n2. **Find B from the sum ν₂ + ν₃.** The sum equals 30Dq + 15B. So 23 430 = 12 210 + 15B, which gives B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹.\n3. **Check with the difference ν₃ − ν₂.** The observed difference is 9370 cm⁻¹. The formula gives √(225·748² + 100·407² − 180·748·407) = √(8.765×10⁷) ≈ 9362 cm⁻¹. This matches the observed value to within about 0.1%, so the band assignment holds up.\n\n**Result**\n\n- Dq ≈ 407 cm⁻¹\n- B ≈ 748 cm⁻¹. This is about 77% of the free-ion value for Co²⁺ (about 970 cm⁻¹), so β ≈ 0.77.\n- C cannot be determined, because the quartet (spin-allowed) transitions do not depend on C.\n\n{\"Dq\": 407, \"B\": 748, \"C\": null, \"out_of_scope\": false, \"reason\": \"Td d7 treated as Oh d3 analogue: 10Dq=ν1, B from ν2+ν3 with ν3−ν2 consistency check.\"}"}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":4,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7974,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":7974,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context